%[text] %[text:anchor:H_C4C21959] ## Random Variables
%[text] A random variable assigns a measurable value to every outcome of a random experiment or a random process. Typically, the measurable values are real numbers.
%[text] **Task.** Open the app `RandomVariableApp` to generate your random variables\*.\*
RandomVariableApp
  %[control:button:7a0a]{"position":[1,2]}
%[text] ![](text:image:7d7c) **Try**
%[text] 1. Follow the instructions in the app to create a new random variable $X${"altText":"X"}. Assign different group of outcomes in the probability space to other real values $n${"altText":"n"} of your choice.
%[text] 2. Based on the classical perspective, what is the probability of $X${"altText":"X"} taking the value $n${"altText":"n"} for every possible value of $n${"altText":"n"}?
%[text] 3. Click **Sample** to generate an empirical distribution of $X${"altText":"X"}; that is, $P(X=n)${"altText":"P(X=n)"} for all $n \\in X${"altText":"n \in X"}. Study the distribution plot.
%[text] 4. Create a random variable in the app's probability space to resemble the following distribution:  \
%[text]  ![Given a random variable X, P of X = 0 is 0.1, P of X = 1 is 0.2, P of X = 2 is 0.4 and P of X = 3 is 0.3](text:image:2478)
%[text]  <u>Example app instance</u>
%[text]  ![Random, uniformly distributed samples of a probability space will eventually allocate themselves proportionately by the area of each option in the sample space.](text:image:9fd3)
%[text] Click the **Stop** button to stop sampling. Change the grid size of the probability space using **Rows** and **Columns** edit fields. Use the **Reset** button to reassign all outcomes to 0 and clear the distribution plot.
%[text] ![](text:image:1387) ***Reflect***
%[text] - *After a large number of samples (say,1000), do the probabilities in the empirical distribution match up with your expectation?* \
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_357E17BB] ### Expectation and Variance
%[text] The distribution of a random variable is often characterized by two parameters—expected value and variance.
%[text] Expected value of a random variable is a measure of the distribution's "center." Mathematically, it is a probability-weighted sum of all the values that the random variable can take.
%[text]{"align":"center"} $E\[X\] = \\sum\_{x\\in X} x P(X=x)${"altText":"E\[X\] = \sum\_{x\in X} x P(X=x)"}
%[text] Variance of a random variable measures the distribution's "spread"; that is, how far the values typically lie from the expected value. Mathematically, it is an average of the squared distances between the values that the variable can take from the random variable's expected value.
%[text]{"align":"center"}  $\\matrix{\\text{Var}\[X\] &=& E\\left\[(X-E\[X\])^2\\right\] \\cr &= &\\sum\_{x\\in X}(X-E(x))^2P(X=x)}${"altText":"Var\[X\] = E\[(X-E\[X\])^2\] = \sum\_{x \in X} (X-E(X))^2 P(X=x)"} 
%[text] **Task.** Open the app `RandomVariableApp` to study the expected value and variance of a random variable.
RandomVariableApp
  %[control:button:9813]{"position":[1,2]}
%[text] ![](text:image:07fb) **Try**
%[text] 1. *Check* ***Show distribution parameters*** *in the app*.
%[text] 2. *Click* ***Load*** $\\pi${"altText":"\pi"} ***example*** *button in the app. Observe the random variable's values and generate its empirical distribution by clicking the* ***Sample*** *button.* \
%[text]  <u>*Example app instance*</u>
%[text]  ![A screenshot from the Random Variable app showing the approximation of pi. Create a 2 by 2 square with an inscribed circle assigned the value of 4 and the remaining area assigned the value of 0. After 123 random samples, the expected value of this variable is 3.122 with variance 2.741](text:image:6b5c)
%[text] ![](text:image:3f5c) ***Reflect***
%[text] - *After a very large number of samples,* *does the expected value of* $X${"altText":"X"}*; that is,* $E\[X\]${"altText":"E\[X\]"}*,* *approach the value of 3.1416? Why?* \
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_D95B268A] ### Discrete Random Variables
%[text] For random processes with discrete outcomes, like the roll of a die or a coin toss, we define a discrete random variable that can take an exact value. The distribution of a discrete random variable is characterized by a *probability mass function* (PMF).
%[text]{"align":"center"} $f(x) = P(X=n)${"altText":"f(x) = P(X=n)"}
%[text] When evaluated at $n${"altText":"n"}, the cumulative distribution function is the probability that the discrete random variable will take a value less than or equal to $n${"altText":"n"}.
%[text]{"align":"center"} $F(x) = P(X\\leq n)${"altText":"F(x) = P(X\leq n)"}
%[text] For example, a discrete uniform random variable follows a distribution where every value is equally likely. This roll of a fair die, the toss of a fair coin, and drawing a single card from a deck can all be described by a uniform distribution.
%[text]{"align":"center"} $f(x) = \\frac{1}{N}${"altText":"f(x) = \frac{1}{N}"}, where $N${"altText":"N"} is the total number of possible discrete values 
%[text] **Example.** If you roll a six-sided die, what is the probability of landing on a number greater than or equal to 4?
%[text] **Task.** Click the **Run** button to visualize the discrete random variable for the roll of a single die.
N = 8;                         % number of possible outcomes %[control:editfield:2c20]{"position":[5,6]}
a = 2;                         % min of interval %[control:editfield:6be2]{"position":[5,6]}
b = 4;                         % max of interval %[control:editfield:3836]{"position":[5,6]}
   %[control:button:723a]{"position":[1,2]}
figure("Position",[0 0 1100 500])
PlotUnifDist(N,a,b);
%[text] ![](text:image:0a1a) **Try**
%[text] - Use the edit fields to set values for `a`, `b`, and `N` to answer this question: What is the probability that an eight-sided die roll will land between 2 and 5 (inclusive)? \
%[text] ![](text:image:390e) ***Reflect***
%[text] - *One of the ways the uniform distribution manifests is when sampling randomly from a population. This approach is often used to approximate the preferences or characteristics of a larger population based on a subset of the population (e.g. election polling). In an ideal scenario, every member of the population is* <u>*equally likely*</u> *to be chosen as a sample. What are some practical considerations of this approach?*
%[text] - *To learn more about other commonly encountered discrete random variables and distributions, refer to the courseware module* [Descriptive Statistics and Probability Distributions](https://www.mathworks.com/matlabcentral/fileexchange/95463-descriptive-stats-and-probability)*.* \
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_0872E05F] ### Continuous Random Variables
%[text] Many random processes have continuous-valued outcomes. For instance, consider the weight of a human of a given age. Because there are infinite possible values, no single numeric value of weight has a non-zero probability of occurring. A continuous random variable never takes on an exact value; that is, $P(X=x)=0${"altText":"P(X=x) = 0"}. For such variables, it makes more sense to specify the probability of falling within a range of values. The probability of the weight being exactly 58 kg is 0. However, there is a finite probability of the weight being between 58 and 58.1 kg.
%[text] The *probability density function (PDF)* is the continuous counterpart to the PMF for discrete variables. However, evaluating the PDF at a specific $x${"altText":"x"} does not give you a valid probability. Instead, these can be interpreted as relative likelihoods. To get a valid probability, you must <u>integrate the PDF</u> over a range of values. 
%[text]{"align":"center"} $P\\left(\\mathrm{x\_1}\<x\<\\mathrm{x\_2}\\right)=\\int\_{\\mathrm{x\_1}}^{\\mathrm{x\_2}} f\\left(x\\right)\\mathrm{d}x\\;${"altText":"P\left(\mathrm{x\_1}\<x\<\mathrm{x\_2}\right)=\int\_{\mathrm{x\_1}}^{\mathrm{x\_2}} f\left(x\right)\mathrm{d}x;"}
%[text] The *cumulative distribution function (CDF), when evaluated at a value* $x${"altText":"x"}, gives the area under the PDF from $-\\infty${"altText":"- \infty"} to $x${"altText":"x"}. In other words, CDF gives the probability that the continuous random variable will take a value less than or equal to $x${"altText":"x"}.
%[text]{"align":"center"} $F(x) = P(X \\leq x)${"altText":"F(x) = P(X \leq x)"}
%[text] For example, the normal distribution or the bell curve is one of the most widely encountered distributions. Its PDF is characterized by the parameters $\\mu${"altText":"\mu"} (mean) and $\\sigma${"altText":"\sigma"} (standard deviation). Different values of these parameters describe different normally distributed random variables.
%[text]{"align":"center"} $f(x) = \\frac{1}{\\sigma \\sqrt{2\\pi}}e^{\\frac{-(x-\\mu)^2}{2\\sigma^2}${"altText":"f(x) = \frac{1}{\sigma \sqrt{2\pi}}e^{\frac{-(x-\mu)^2}{2\sigma^2}"}
%[text] **Example.** The average monthly salary of government employees in a certain town is \\$2600, with a standard deviation of \\$500. If you pick an employee at random, what is the probability their salary is between \\$3000 and \\$4000?
%[text] **Task.** Click the **Run** button to visualize the continuous random variable for the salaries of government employees.
mu    = 2600;           % parameter of the distribution %[control:editfield:300a]{"position":[9,13]}
sigma = 500;           % parameter of the distribution %[control:editfield:6ce2]{"position":[9,12]}
x1    = 3000;           % min of interval %[control:editfield:4e46]{"position":[9,13]}
x2    = 4000;           % max of interval %[control:editfield:122a]{"position":[9,13]}
   %[control:button:900b]{"position":[1,2]}
figure("Position",[0 0 1100 500])
PlotNormDist(mu,sigma,x1,x2)
%[text] ![](text:image:131a) ***Reflect***
%[text] - *What is the maximum possible value that any cumulative distribution function can produce? Why?*
%[text] - *To learn more about other commonly encountered continuous random variables and distributions, refer to the courseware module* [Descriptive Statistics and Probability Distributions](https://www.mathworks.com/matlabcentral/fileexchange/95463-descriptive-stats-and-probability)*.* \
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_EC1B98E4] ## Central Limit Theorem
%[text] The Central Limit Theorem provides the foundational basis for drawing inferences from unknown random processes by sampling. The theorem says that the averages of samples taken from a large population follow an approximately normal distribution, regardless of the shape and type of the underlying population distribution. This is why the normal distribution or the bell-shaped curve is ubiquitous in mathematics, statistics, and data science.
%[text] As the sample size gets larger, the distribution of the sample averages tends towards a narrow and perfectly normal distribution. This normal distribution of sample means is also called the *sampling distribution*. With larger sample sizes, the mean of the *sampling distribution* starts approaching the true population mean.
%[text] **Task.** Click the **Run** button to simulate the central limit theorem with a known underlying population distribution. Verify whether the fitted normal distribution PDF (red) is a good representation of the histogram (pink).
alpha =    3;       % parameter of the underlying gamma distributed population %[control:slider:56f0]{"position":[12,13]}
beta =      2;       % parameter of the underlying gamma distributed population %[control:slider:4184]{"position":[13,14]}
NumSamples =30;        % number of samples %[control:slider:0ef5]{"position":[13,15]}
NumDraws =  80;       % number of draws %[control:slider:4ddf]{"position":[13,15]}
  %[control:button:0e09]{"position":[1,2]}
figure("Position",[0 0 600 400])
CentralLimitTheorem(alpha,beta,NumSamples,NumDraws)
%[text] ![](text:image:676e) **Try**
%[text] 1. Decrease the number of samples to `10` using the slider for `NumSamples`, and click **Run**`.` How well does the normal distribution PDF represent the histogram of sample means?
%[text] 2. Increase the number of samples to `50` using the slider for `NumSamples`, and click **Run**. How well does the normal distribution PDF represent the histogram of sample means?
%[text] 3. Modify the underlying population distribution by changing the parameters `alpha` and `beta`. Repeat the experiment. \
%[text] ![](text:image:63be) ***Reflect***
%[text] - *Are there any conditions of the underlying population distribution where the central limit theorem does not seem to hold, even with a large number of samples?* \
